  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nRainfall moving across the area is pretty much coming in as \nforecast, with a broad deformation band of rain to the northwest of \na surface low that is near the IA/MO/IL border this afternoon. \nRainfall amounts are on track to come in in the 0.75\" to 1\" range \nfrom down around Albert Lea up toward Chippewa Falls/Eau Claire, \naround 0.5\" in the Twin Cities, down to around 0.1\" by the time you \nget to St. Cloud. This rain shield will clear the Eau Claire area \nbetween 10pm and midnight, to be replaced by a tight pressure \ngradient and strong low level CAA. This will allow the winds to kick \nup, with wind gusts up over 40 mph expected west of I-35 from \nroughly midnight to noon on Friday before they start to slowly \nsettle down in the afternoon. Besides the winds, forcing from the \nnorthern stream shortwave, combined with instability from the \ndifferential CAA, will result in a batch of showers moving south \nthrough MN around midnight (as seen with the HRRR). After that, it's \nlooking like this may be the last chance for rain we have for the \nnext 7+ days.\n\nFriday, high pressure will come out of the Rockies and sink \nsoutheast toward the southern Plains. The ridge axis will be \ncentered over the MPX area between 00z and 06z Saturday (7pm Friday \nto 1am Saturday) and will already be off to our southeast by \nSaturday morning. This leads to some uncertainty with lows Saturday \nmorning, especially over western and central MN, where return flow \nand southwest winds will start blowing during the night, with lows \nlikely being achieved near midnight, with temperatures remaining \nsteady or rising through the rest of the night. Still, NBM \nprobability for a low of 32 or less is basically 100% northeast of I-\n94 and 50-75% southwest of I-94 (for the core of the Twin Cities \nmetro, the freeze probability drops to 20% thanks to the urban heat \nisland), so we went ahead with a Freeze Watch for the entire area \nFriday night/Saturday morning. Growing Season Index (GSI) values are \ncurrently up around 0.8, which indicates stressed, but still active \nvegetation. By Thursday next week, the GSI values are forecast to \nfall below 0.5, indicating that this brief cold push combined with \ndecreasing daylight will be enough to shut down the growing season \nfor 2026.\n\nFor the rest of the forecast, it's back to ridging and dry Pacific \nairmasses. One thing we've seen is that models are holding this \nridging pattern in place longer, with any sort of breakdown in the \npattern not coming until next weekend or early the following week. \nIt will still be cool on Saturday thanks to the cold start, but from \nSunday through next week, it will be highs in the mid 60s to lower \n70s as our exceptionally warm October weather returns after this \nbrief cool down. The one thing to watch starting Saturday and \ncontinuing through next week will be the potential for elevated fire \nweather conditions. Right now, the day with the lowest dewpoints \nalso has our coolest temperatures, with the warmer temperatures next \nweek coming with higher dewpoints. This all works out to RHs \nremaining above critical thresholds (25%), but we'll have to keep a \nclose eye on RHs given the potential for some breezy periods to \ngo with this unseasonably mild airmass.",
